1	// pattern: Mixed (unavoidable)
2	//
3	// Client-side Shamir share generation for the DID ceremony (the ceremony inversion):
4	// the wallet — not the server — generates the recovery seed, derives the recovery
5	// rotation key from it, and splits the seed 2-of-3 into v2 share envelopes. Custos
6	// receives exactly one share (the Share 2 envelope, deposited with the ceremony
7	// request) and never sees the seed or the other shares, so no server backup can ever
8	// hold reconstruction material.
9	//
10	// Retry resilience lives here too: the generated set is persisted in a Keychain
11	// staging slot (`ceremony-staging` — transient working material, distinct from the
12	// durable `recovery-share-1` slot) BEFORE any network call, so a retry reuses the
13	// identical set (same set_id) instead of orphaning a prior attempt's escrow deposit.
14	// The staging record holds the three envelopes only — the seed is recomputed from
15	// Shares 1+2 on load, and every load re-validates each envelope's checksum and
16	// cross-checks set_ids. A present-but-unreadable record **fails closed**
17	// (`StagingCorrupt`) rather than regenerating: it may be the exact set a prior
18	// attempt already bound to a genesis op and escrowed, so overwriting it would
19	// permanently destroy the recovery seed. Only a genuinely absent slot — or one
20	// staged for different ceremony inputs, which a new ceremony explicitly abandons —
21	// permits fresh generation.
22	//
23	// Teardown order is load-bearing: `clear_staging` runs only after Share 1 has
24	// verifiably reached its durable slot and the user has confirmed saving Share 3
25	// (`confirm_share_backup` in lib.rs drives that), because until then the staging
26	// slot is the only durable home of the seed material.
